{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Draw out and take for yourselves.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: as you drew the sheep for the mitzvah, I will draw your enemies to the Sea of Reeds and drown them in it; and by this merit (Judges 4:7): ״ומשכתי אליך אל נחל קישון את סיסרא״. Another interpretation: because the descent of our forefathers to Egypt was by means of drawing, as it says (Genesis 37:28): ״וימשכו ויעלו את יוסף״, and concerning them it says (Isaiah 5:18): ״הוי מושכי העון בחבלי השוא״, and with this expression their punishment was written, as it says (Nehemiah 9:30): ״ותמשוך עליהם שנים רבות״, and Israel are called (Isaiah 18:7): ״עם ממושך וממורט״, and when they return in repentance it is said of them (Psalms 36:11): ״משוך חסדך ליודעיך״, and it is written (Song of Songs 1:4): ״משכני אחריך נרוצה״, and it is written (Hosea 11:4): ״בחבלי אדם אמשכם בעבותות אהבה״." "<b>For your families.</b> From here [we learn] that a person is obligated to bring near his relatives, so that they may rejoice with them on the festivals; and so in the days of the redemption, the Holy One, blessed be He, promised us that each man will draw near to his family, as it says (Jeremiah 30:8): ״בעת ההיא נאם ה' אהיה לאלהים לכל משפחות ישראל״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The second prooftext is cited as Judges 4:7 but the quoted phrase ומשכתי אליך אל נחל קישון את סיסרא does not correspond to the received text of Judges 4:7; citation retained as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:13.994897083Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}